---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Sebastian Correa-Gallego"
  ogType="profile"
>
  <div class="container">

    <header class="hero">
      <p class="hero__eyebrow">Biologist · Universidad EAFIT · Medellín, Colombia</p>
      <img
        src="/assets/profile.jpg"
        alt="Portrait of Sebastian Correa-Gallego"
        class="hero__photo"
        width="116"
        height="116"
        loading="eager"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <h1 class="hero__name">Sebastian Correa-Gallego</h1>
      <p class="hero__subtitle">Bachelor of Science in Biology</p>
      <p class="hero__statement">
        I study how biological systems <em>assemble, organize, and transform</em>
        across time and environment — and whether the forms life takes are written
        by rule, or by history and chance.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section id="orientation" class="block">
      <h2 class="heading reveal"><span class="heading__i">01</span><span class="heading__t">Orientation</span></h2>
      <div class="prose reveal">
        <p>
          My work approaches a single question from two directions. The outward window
          looks at microbial communities in natural systems — who is present, why, and what
          changes when the environment shifts. The inward window looks at cellular physiology
          and evolutionary transitions — how organisms reorganize their resources and functions
          under constraint. The long-term aim is to work at the interface: ecological questions
          given the analytical depth they require, without abandoning the natural history that
          generated them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Microbial systems are the platform — tractable timescales, replicable experiments,
          and environmental gradients that serve as natural laboratories for asking what
          determines which forms of life emerge under which conditions.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="pull reveal">
        <span class="pull__label">Current direction</span>
        <span class="pull__text">
          Cellular resource allocation and the predictability of microbial community assembly.
        </span>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="research" class="block">
      <h2 class="heading reveal"><span class="heading__i">02</span><span class="heading__t">Research</span></h2>
      <p class="lede reveal">Two windows on one question.</p>

      <article class="card reveal">
        <span class="card__meta">Outward · Microbial communities</span>
        <h3 class="card__title card__title--italic">
          Cultivable Microbial Community Structure Along a Light Gradient in a Tropical
          Volcaniclastic Cave
        </h3>
        <p class="card__sub">Undergraduate thesis · Universidad EAFIT · Advisor: Prof. Nicolás Pinel Peláez</p>
        <p class="card__desc">
          In a tropical volcaniclastic cave, a light gradient runs from the entrance to the
          aphotic zone. Across it, the same cave — under different energetic conditions —
          produced structurally different microbial communities. Environment as sculptor,
          community as response; the gradient as the axis that makes the relationship visible.
        </p>
        <a class="card__link" href="https://hdl.handle.net/10784/38213" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
          Read the thesis · EAFIT repository →
        </a>
      </article>

      <article class="card reveal">
        <span class="card__meta">Inward · Cellular constraint</span>
        <h3 class="card__title">Proteome allocation in osmotrophic yeasts under carbon limitation</h3>
        <p class="card__sub">ECSO Lab, Purdue University · Visiting Student Intern, 2025–2026</p>
        <p class="card__desc">
          How does a cell reorganize its own molecular machinery to survive a different energetic
          regime? Working with yeast under carbon limitation, this project follows proteome
          allocation as resources change — the same logic as the cave, at the scale of a single
          organism. A manuscript is in preparation.
        </p>
      </article>
    </section>

    <section id="presentations" class="block">
      <h2 class="heading reveal"><span class="heading__i">03</span><span class="heading__t">Presentations</span></h2>
      <div class="reveal">
        <div class="entry">
          <div class="entry__row">
            <span class="entry__title">Research proposal presentation</span>
            <span class="entry__date">2024</span>
          </div>
          <p class="entry__sub">2nd Symposium of Biology, Universidad EAFIT · <em>Awarded second place</em></p>
        </div>
        <div class="entry">
          <div class="entry__row">
            <span class="entry__title">Oral presentation</span>
            <span class="entry__date">2024</span>
          </div>
          <p class="entry__sub">XXIII Encuentro Departamental de Semilleros de Investigación, RedCOLSI, Antioquia</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="honors" class="block">
      <h2 class="heading reveal"><span class="heading__i">04</span><span class="heading__t">Honors &amp; Recognition</span></h2>
      <div class="reveal">
        <div class="entry">
          <div class="entry__row">
            <span class="entry__title">Fully funded visiting internship</span>
            <span class="entry__date">2025</span>
          </div>
          <p class="entry__sub">Fundación Fraternidad Medellín — UREP-C Program</p>
        </div>
        <div class="entry">
          <div class="entry__row">
            <span class="entry__title">Undergraduate scholarship</span>
            <span class="entry__date">2022</span>
          </div>
          <p class="entry__sub">Comfama and Fundación Fraternidad Medellín</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="record" class="block">
      <h2 class="heading reveal"><span class="heading__i">05</span><span class="heading__t">Academic Record</span></h2>
      <div class="reveal">
        <div class="entry">
          <div class="entry__row">
            <span class="entry__title">B.Sc. in Biology</span>
            <span class="entry__date">2022–2026</span>
          </div>
          <p class="entry__sub">Universidad EAFIT · GPA 4.44 / 5.00</p>
        </div>
        <div class="entry">
          <div class="entry__row">
            <span class="entry__title">Visiting Student Intern</span>
            <span class="entry__date">Aug 2025 – Jan 2026</span>
          </div>
          <p class="entry__sub">ECSO Lab, Department of Biological Sciences, Purdue University</p>
        </div>
        <div class="entry">
          <div class="entry__row">
            <span class="entry__title">Undergraduate Thesis Researcher</span>
            <span class="entry__date">2024–2026</span>
          </div>
          <p class="entry__sub">Universidad EAFIT · Advisor: Prof. Nicolás Pinel Peláez</p>
        </div>
        <div class="entry">
          <div class="entry__row">
            <span class="entry__title">Student Director, Microbiology &amp; Astrobiology Group (SIAB)</span>
            <span class="entry__date">Oct 2023 – May 2025</span>
          </div>
          <p class="entry__sub">Universidad EAFIT · affiliated with GEBI</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="profile" class="block">
      <h2 class="heading reveal"><span class="heading__i">06</span><span class="heading__t">Technical Profile</span></h2>
      <div class="prose reveal">
        <p>
          My training spans cultivation-based microbiology, environmental sampling, microscopy,
          and morphotype characterization from fieldwork in subterranean systems, together with
          cell physiology and continuous-culture workflows developed at Purdue.
        </p>
        <p>
          I complement laboratory and field practice with quantitative analysis in R and Python,
          data visualization, and reproducible, literate workflows — progressively integrating
          formal methods with the natural history that motivates them.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</Base>
